title: Pulmonary neuroendocrine tumor classification
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key decision:'' Separate well-differentiated carcinoids from high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas before choosing treatment.
* ''Typical carcinoid:'' Well-differentiated tumor with <2 mitoses per 2 mm² and no necrosis.
* ''Atypical carcinoid:'' Well-differentiated morphology with 2–10 mitoses per 2 mm² or focal necrosis.
** Compared with typical carcinoid, nodal spread and distant metastases are more likely.
* ''Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma:'' High-grade tumor with >10 mitoses per 2 mm².
** Organoid nesting, palisading, or rosettes accompany large cells and appreciable cytoplasm.
** Prominent nucleoli and less nuclear molding help distinguish it from small cell carcinoma.
* ''Small cell lung cancer:'' Small cells, scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and inconspicuous nucleoli.
** Numerous mitoses (>10 per 2 mm²), extensive necrosis, and crush artifact are characteristic.
* ''Markers:'' Synaptophysin, chromogranin, INSM1, and CD56 support neuroendocrine differentiation alongside morphology.
** A positive neuroendocrine stain alone does not establish small cell carcinoma.
* ''Counting:'' Use calibrated 2-mm² areas; ten high-power fields are not universally equivalent.
* ''Ki-67:'' Helps distinguish crushed carcinoid from small cell carcinoma; morphology and mitoses remain central.
* ''Pitfall:'' Discordant small biopsies warrant expert thoracic pathology review before committing to treatment.
** Carcinoids can occur without smoking; both high-grade carcinomas are strongly smoking-associated.

Sources: ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 11, pp. 529–530, Table 11-1 (PDF 536–537); NCCN Small Cell Lung Cancer v1.2027, SCL-B 1, Discussion MS-5.
